    "body": "Sphera has announced new artificial intelligence tools for its Supply Chain Risk Management software to help companies link external disruptions to the affected suppliers, products and operations.\nWhat’s Related\nThe additions include conversational AI, a connection to compatible AI tools, and AI agents that assess risk severity and supplier impact. Sphera says the tools are designed to shorten the time between detecting a threat and deciding how to respond.\nThe launch follows a survey of 750 chief operating officers across the United States, United Kingdom, Germany, France, and United Arab Emirates. It found that 58% said their most significant disruptions originated at least as much outside their organizations as within them. That figure reached 71% in the UAE.\nThe research also identified specific response challenges. Identifying exposed suppliers or products was cited by 33% of respondents, followed by choosing the right response (30%) and measuring the business impact (29%).\nRELATED: Supply Chain Disruptions Are Quietly Hurting Company Revenue\nBringing risk management\nThe new conversational AI features let users ask questions about supplier and supply chain risk information in plain language. According to Sphera, the features show which suppliers, products and operations are exposed, reducing the need to manually assemble information from multiple views.\nThe company also added a Model Context Protocol connection, which lets organizations bring information from their risk management software into compatible AI tools. That gives teams another way to access the information when making decisions.\nWithin Sphera’s Risk Response & Resolution Center, AI agents evaluate risk severity and supplier impact to help teams identify issues requiring a response. The company says this is intended to reduce the manual work of reviewing and prioritizing alerts.\nConfidence gap\nThe survey found a gap between executives’ confidence and their ability to quickly assess disruptions. While COOs expressed confidence in their understanding of business dependencies, only 5% said they could assess the impact of an external event within minutes.\n“The boundary of the enterprise is no longer the boundary of operational risk,” said Paul Marushka, CEO and president of Sphera.\nAll 750 executives surveyed identified at least one risk management activity they would allow AI to advance automatically , provided appropriate rules and controls were in place.",
